{
  "id": "Compound::DB00477 - Gene::1129",
  "verbalization": "Chlorpromazine's interaction with Chrm2 is supported by multiple pathways that converge on the G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity. The drug binds to Hrh4, which is involved in the regulation of G-protein coupled receptors, and this interaction is linked to the participation of Chrm2 in G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity. This suggests that Chlorpromazine may modulate Chrm2 through a mechanism involving the G-protein signaling cascade. The evidence from the three paths is consistent, with varying scores reflecting the strength of the interactions, but all pointing to a shared biological pathway. The mechanism appears to involve the modulation of acetylcholine receptor activity, potentially through the influence of Hrh4 on G-protein signaling. The integration of these pathways supports a plausible mechanism by which Chlorpromazine could influence Chrm2, though further experimental validation would be necessary to confirm the exact nature of the interaction.",
